{
  "description": "issue #688 (terminal slice, review round 1): the `every` half of the Core numeric-word rule (`spec/execution-model.md:33-34`, `Words that parse as numbers are accepted where a number is expected`). `every \"2\"` registers a handler with a tick count of 2 and behaves identically to the bare `every 2`: the handler first runs 2 ticks after registration and repeats every 2 ticks while the `wait 4` pause advances the clock, firing twice. Both of `every`'s numeric checks are therefore proven to run on the COERCED value, not to reject the word before they are reached -- the wholeness and range checks still apply to it (`every \"2.5\"` would be `ol-type` and `every \"0\"` `ol-range`, by the same rule that makes this legal). This is the positive complement of `every-non-number-type-error`, without which an implementation rejecting every word would pass the corpus.",
